WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.766
How could we take
this incredible capability,

00:00:01.766 --> 00:00:06.666
the finesse of human actions
and finesse of using our hand,

00:00:06.666 --> 00:00:11.066
but apply it
to the virtual world?

00:00:11.066 --> 00:00:14.900
We use radio frequency spectrum,
which is radars,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.066
to track human hand.

00:00:17.066 --> 00:00:18.000
Radars have been used
for many different things--

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.033
Lien: Our team is focused
on taking radar hardware

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.133
and turning it
into a gesture sensor.

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:25.766
Radar is a technology

00:00:25.766 --> 00:00:28.966
which transmits a radio wave
towards a target,

00:00:28.966 --> 00:00:30.733
and then the receiver
of the radar

00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:34.000
intercepts the reflected energy
from that target.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.833
which means that you can sense
the tiniest motions.

00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:38.900
Schwesig: We arrived
at this idea of virtual tools

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.400
because we recognized
that there are

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.800
certain archetypes of controls,
like a volume knob

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.633
or a physical slider,
a volume slider,

00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:49.100
Imagine a button

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.733
between your thumb
and your index finger,

00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:53.566
and the button's not there,

00:00:53.566 --> 00:00:56.166
but pressing this
is a very clear action.

00:00:56.166 --> 00:00:58.366
And there's an actual
physical haptic feedback

00:00:58.366 --> 00:01:00.500
that occurs as you perform
that action.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.733
The hand can both embody
a virtual tool,

00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:05.466
and it can also be, you know,

00:01:05.466 --> 00:01:07.666
acting on that virtual tool
at the same time.

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:09.700
So if we can recognize
that action,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.000
we have an interesting direction
for interacting with technology.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:10.466
It can work through materials.

00:01:10.466 --> 00:01:12.066
You can embed it into objects.

00:01:12.066 --> 00:01:14.033
It allows us to track
really precise motions.

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:15.833
And what is most exciting
about it

00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:18.300
is that you can shrink
the entire radar

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.000
and put it in a tiny chip.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.800
That's what makes this approach
so promising.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.333
It's extremely reliable.

00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:25.600
There's nothing to break.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.000
There's no moving parts.
